.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.\" Copyright (C) 2026 Kevin H. Patterson
.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt UNZAR 1
.Os zar @PROJECT_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm unzar
.Nd extract, list, test, or scan a zar archive
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar archive
.Op Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
extracts an archive made by
.Xr zar 1 ,
verifying every chunk as it goes.
If
.Ar archive
is
.Ql - ,
it is read from standard input: at random if that is a file, otherwise as
a stream, which works for archives made with
.Nm zar Fl \-head-index .
.Pp
If any
.Ar path
is given, only those entries are processed, with everything below the
ones that are directories.
.Pp
.Nm
runs in one of three modes:
.Bl -tag -width "-t, --test" -offset indent
.It Fl x , Fl \-extract
writes the files, directories, and links
.It Fl t , Fl \-test
decompresses and verifies everything, and writes nothing
.It Fl \-scan
checks the archive's structure, checksums, and sizes without
decompressing, and writes nothing
.El
.Pp
At most one may be given.
Without any,
.Nm
extracts, or with
.Fl \-list ,
scans: so
.Nm
.Fl l
lists an archive without writing anything, and
.Nm
.Fl lx
lists it while extracting.
.Pp
Nothing is extracted if anything to be extracted already exists, unless
.Fl y
is given.
A file that fails a checksum or size check is removed rather than left
half-written, and the rest of the archive is still extracted.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C , Fl \-directory Ns = Ns Ar dir
Extract into
.Ar dir ,
creating it if need be.
The default is the current directory.
.It Fl l , Fl \-list
Print a listing: each directory on its own line, followed by its files with
their size and modification time, separated by tabs.
Control characters, backslashes, and bytes of names that aren't UTF-8 are
escaped.
.It Fl x , Fl \-extract
Extract.
This is the default except with
.Fl l .
.It Fl \-scan
Check everything except decompression: block and index checksums, sizes,
and paths.
This is the default with
.Fl l .
.It Fl t , Fl \-test
Like
.Fl \-scan ,
but also decompress and verify all data.
.It Fl y , Fl \-yes
Overwrite files and directories that already exist.
.It Fl \-dirs Ns = Ns Ar mode
What to do with directories that already exist:
.Cm merge
(the default) extracts into them;
.Cm replace
first deletes each one that is extracted whole, but never the parents of a
selected
.Ar path .
Either needs
.Fl y .
.It Fl \-hard-links
Recreate hard links as links, where their targets are extracted too.
By default each is extracted as an independent copy.
.It Fl \-sparse Ns = Ns Ar mode
How to write the zeros in sparse files:
.Cm keep
(the default) leaves holes, unallocated, wherever the archive records them;
.Cm auto
also leaves a hole wherever a whole chunk is zeros; and
.Cm never
writes every byte, for files that must be fully allocated.
Holes are kept a chunk at a time, so smaller ones are written as zeros.
.It Fl j , Fl \-threads Ns Op = Ns Ar n
Use
.Ar n
worker threads, from 1 to 1024.
The default, and the value without
.Ar n ,
is one per CPU core.
.It Fl \-max-size Ns = Ns Ar size
Refuse to decompress more than
.Ar size
bytes in all.
The selected files' total is checked before anything is written, and a
solid archive stops decompressing once it passes
.Ar size .
Without this option, extraction is limited to the free space at the
destination, and a solid archive to the free space in
.Ev TMPDIR .
Holes left as holes count toward neither limit.
.It Fl h , Fl \-human-readable
Show sizes as 512, 1.0K, 31.4M, 2.0G, and so on.
.It Fl v , Fl \-verbose
With
.Fl l ,
also show each entry's permissions and owner.
Print a summary at the end.
.It Fl \-help
Show a summary of the options.
.It Fl \-version
Show the version, the archive format version, and the zstd version.
.El
.Pp
Short options can be combined, as in
.Fl lxv .
The count for
.Fl j
must be attached, as in
.Fl j4 .
Sizes take an optional
.Cm K ,
.Cm M ,
.Cm G ,
or
.Cm T
suffix, for binary multiples.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where a large solid archive is decompressed to.
The default is
.Pa /tmp .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
Finished, but some entries had problems, each reported on standard error.
.It 2
A fatal error: bad usage, an unreadable or invalid archive, existing
targets without
.Fl y ,
and so on.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Extract
.Pa proj.zar
into the current directory:
.Dl $ unzar proj.zar
.Pp
Extract one directory from it, elsewhere:
.Dl $ unzar -C /tmp/out proj.zar proj/src
.Pp
List an archive with human-readable sizes, checking it as it goes:
.Dl $ unzar -lh proj.zar
.Pp
List it while extracting it:
.Dl $ unzar -lx proj.zar
.Pp
Replace an earlier extraction:
.Dl $ unzar -y --dirs=replace proj.zar
.Pp
Extract an archive as it downloads:
.Dl $ curl -s https://example.com/proj.zar | unzar -
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zar 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kevin H. Patterson
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Nm
is meant to be safe on untrusted archives.
It rejects absolute paths and paths with
.Ql \&.. ,
.Ql \&. ,
or empty components, and never writes through a symbolic link: files are
created one path component at a time without following links, and the
archive's own symbolic links are created last.
Ownership is restored only when running as root, and set-user-ID and
set-group-ID bits only along with it.
Names are escaped whenever they are printed.
.Pp
The CRC-32C checksums detect accidental damage, not tampering.
